description:
Build the request side of an HTTP/1.1 server: a parser that takes bytes as they arrive from a connection and produces complete requests, with chunked bodies, pipelining, keep-alive and the limits of lesson 3.02, plus a correct response builder.

```cpp
using Headers = std::vector<std::pair<std::string, std::string>>;
struct Request { std::string method, target, version; Headers headers; std::string body; bool keepAlive; };

class RequestParser
{
public:
    RequestParser(std::size_t maxHead, std::size_t maxBody);
    void feed(const std::string& bytes);
    std::optional<Request> next();
    bool failed() const;
    int errorStatus() const;
    std::size_t pending() const;
};

std::string buildResponse(int status, const Headers& headers, const std::string& body);
```

**The head.** It ends at the first `\r\n\r\n`. If no terminator has arrived and more than `maxHead` bytes are buffered, or the head is longer than `maxHead`, the parser fails with **431**. The head is parsed as in practice 3.02 (names in lower case, values trimmed), except that the version must be exactly `HTTP/1.0` or `HTTP/1.1`. Any malformed head fails with **400**.

**The body.** Decided as in practice 3.02: an invalid combination fails with **400**; `Content-Length` larger than `maxBody` fails with **413**. A **chunked** body is a series of chunks: a size line of 1 to 8 hex digits (optionally followed by `;` and an extension, which is ignored) ending `\r\n`, that many bytes, and `\r\n`; a size of 0 ends the chunks, and is followed by optional trailer lines and an empty line (`\r\n`). Malformed chunks fail with **400**; a decoded body larger than `maxBody` fails with **413**.

**Keep-alive.** For `HTTP/1.1` it is true unless a `Connection` header contains the token `close`; for `HTTP/1.0` it is false unless a `Connection` header contains `keep-alive` (tokens are separated by commas, compared ignoring case and surrounding spaces).

**The parser.** `feed` appends bytes; `next` returns the next complete request (several can be waiting, pipelined) or `std::nullopt` if more bytes are needed. Once failed, the parser ignores further input and `next` always gives `std::nullopt`; `errorStatus` says why (0 while not failed). `pending` is the number of buffered bytes not yet consumed.

**buildResponse** writes `HTTP/1.1 CODE REASON\r\n` (reasons as in practice 3.01), each header as `Name: value\r\n` in order, then `Content-Length: N\r\n` unless the status is 1xx, 204 or 304 (which have no body: the body is then left out), then `\r\n` and the body.

